{"ts":"2026-07-06T20:32:44.238Z","author":"codex-cli-agent","op":"history_compact_baseline","patch":[{"op":"replace","path":"/body","value":"# Semgrep finding import\n\nSemgrep finding id: 874840457\nRule: javascript.lang.security.audit.detect-non-literal-regexp.detect-non-literal-regexp\nProduct: Code\nSeverity: Medium\nStatus: Open\nConfidence: Low\nCategory: security\nIs Pro Rule: True\nSemgrep autotriage result: False positive\nSemgrep file risk tag: Unspecified\nRepository: unbraind/pm-cli\nBranch: refs/heads/main\nCreated at: 2026-07-03 12:04:25.346308\nLast opened at: 2026-07-03 12:04:25.327926\n\nLinks:\n- Code: https://github.com/unbraind/pm-cli/blob/6f511706628f18743a00a1cfe851e8d1f492684e/src/cli/commands/validate.ts#L1210\n- Semgrep: https://semgrep.dev/orgs/unbrained/findings/874840457\n- Repository: https://github.com/unbraind/pm-cli\n\nRule description:\nRegExp() called with a `params` function argument, this might allow an attacker to cause a Regular Expression Denial-of-Service (ReDoS) within your application as RegExP blocks the main thread. For this reason, it is recommended to use hardcoded regexes instead. If your regex is run on user-controlled input, consider performing input validation or use a regex checking/sanitization library such as https://www.npmjs.com/package/recheck to verify that the regex does not appear vulnerable to ReDoS.\n\nAutotriage reason:\nThe regex pattern is built from a configurable prefix that is first normalized and then escaped with an escaping helper before being passed to RegExp, so regex metacharacters from the argument cannot change the pattern. This is in CLI validation code and the match appears to come from the rule treating any function parameter as tainted, not from an actually exploitable ReDoS case.\n\nImported from downloaded CSV:\nSemgrep_Code_Combined_Findings_2026_07_06.csv (local download, not committed)"},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/id","value":"pm-o61m"},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/title","value"